1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Bc4 Nf6 5. O-O d6 $6 ({Very meek.} 5... Nxe4 {has been universally played, virtually guaranteeing Black equality after} 6. Re1 d5 $10 {Bernard Cafferty wrote in the Birmingham Mail: "Having watched both players from a ringside seat, I am sure Smyslov lost this game because he had this fixed idea that he had to win at all costs to keep in the running for first prize. He deliberately chose an old-fashioned opening variation and avoided simplification at all costs so as to rule out easy equality. Nor did he offer a draw at any point in the game, although such an offer might well have saved him around move 18 or so."}) 6. Nxd4 Be7 7. Nc3 O-O 8. b3 (8. Re1 Bd7 9. f4 Nxd4 10. Qxd4 c6 11. h3 Qc7 12. Qf2 b5 13. Bd3 Ne8 14. e5 b4 15. Ne4 {Tolush-Kopayev, USSR 1938}) (8. h3 Nxd4 9. Qxd4 Be6 10. Bxe6 fxe6 11. e5 Nd7 12. exd6 {Tarrasch-Taubenhaus, Ostend 1905}) 8... Re8 (8... Nxd4 $142 {?Be6}) 9. Bb2 Bf8 10. Re1 Bd7 11. Qd2 Ne5 12. Bf1 g6 13. h3 Bg7 14. Rad1 Nc6 15. Nf3 a6 16. a4 h6 17. Ba1 Kh7 18. Nh2 Nb4 19. f4 Bc6 20. Bc4 Kg8 21. a5 b5 $2 ({Immediately after the game [Smyslov] told us he should have played} 21... Qb8 $142 {with ...b5 in prospect and ...Qa7+ as a stand-by in some lines of play.}) 22. axb6 cxb6 23. Qxd6 Qxd6 24. Rxd6 Nxe4 25. Rxg6 $1 (25. Rxc6 $2 Bxc3) (25. Nxe4 $2 Bxe4) 25... Nf6 26. Ne4 Rxe4 27. Bxf6 $1 (27. Rxe4 Bxe4 28. Rxg7+ Kxg7 29. Ng4 Nxc2 $17) 27... Rxe1+ 28. Kf2 Re6 $2 (28... Nxc2 29. Rxg7+ Kf8 (29... Kh8 30. Rxf7+ Kg8 31. Rg7+ Kh8 32. Re7#) 30. Ng4 $16 (30. Rh7 $1 Ke8 31. Bxf7+ Kd7 32. Bg6+ Ke6 33. Bxc2 Rc1 34. f5+ $1 $18)) 29. Rxg7+ Kf8 30. Bxe6 fxe6 31. Rc7 $18 Nd5 (31... Re8 32. Bc3 $18) 32. Rxc6 Nxf6 33. Rxe6 Kf7 34. Rxb6 a5 35. Ng4 Nd5 36. Rxh6 Ke7 37. g3 Nb4 38. c3 Nd3+ 39. Ke3 Nc5 40. Rb6 Rd8 41. Rb5 $2 ({Corden has long ceased to write down his moves and makes this move to be quite sure he has passed the time control, overlooking the obviously superior} 41. Ne5 $1 {Smyslov could have resigned rather than extend the game to a second session (CHESS)}) 41... Rd3+ 42. Ke2 Rxc3 43. Ne3 Kd6 44. Rxa5 Rxb3 45. Ra3 Rb1 46. Kf3 Nb3 47. Kg4 Kc5 48. Ra8 Nd2 49. Re8 Kd4 50. Nf5+ Kd3 51. Re3+ Kc4 52. h4 Nf1 53. Re8 Nh2+ 54. Kh3 Nf3 55. h5 {Sources: CHESS, February 1970, Vol.35/597-8, p174; BCM, February 1970, p59} 1-0

