{LITTLEWOOD'S CHOICE [Surrounded as I am by all these talented young players such as Howell, Haslinger, Gordon, Jones and many others, may I introduce a touch of nostalgia in this month's article as I remember one of my own exciting games from thirty-eight years ago!? In it, I commit myself to my longest combination ever ...]} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Bc4 d6 5. Nxd4 {[Martyn had already beaten Smyslov in this line, so I switch to my favourite version of Philidor's Defence instead of entering the Antoshin variation involving ...Be7 which Smyslov had tried.]} g6 {[Now that White has committed himself to Bc4, this fianchetto development is less dangerous for Black.]} 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Be3 Nf6 8. f3 O-O 9. Qd2 ({Here if anywhere there may have been an improvement for White. On the principle that the side with more space should avoid exchanges} 9. Nde2 {was worth consideration, maintaining control of d5. (JEL database)}) 9... Ne5 $5 {[#] [A bit of bluffing is involved here, as I force White to choose where to play his bishop. If he wishes to castle long, he is worried about placing it on b3 when it is exposed to a pawn advance by ...a6 and ...c5, but of course he could instead castle short, when he gives up the idea of an immediate attack on my king.]} 10. Be2 $6 d5 $1 {[Perfectly logical and equalising at once.]} 11. f4 $2 {[This reckless advance is a sign that Martyn is unwilling to accept he no longer has an advantage.]} Neg4 12. e5 Nxe3 13. Qxe3 c5 $1 {[The point, as in a number of similar situations.]} 14. exf6 cxd4 ({Even stronger would have been} 14... Re8 15. Qf3 cxd4 16. fxg7 dxc3 17. bxc3 Qe7 {My move gives me a winning game, but Corden defends resourcefully, not being afraid to sacrifice pawns in order to obtain counter-play. (JEL Database)}) 15. Qxd4 Bxf6 16. Qd2 ({Not of course} 16. Qxd5 $4 Bxc3+ {winning the queen.}) 16... Be6 17. O-O {The best chance, even though he loses two pawns. On the other hand, it would be suicide to castle on the queenside when Black has an easy mating attack. (JEL Database)} Qb6+ 18. Kh1 Qxb2 19. Nxd5 Bxd5 20. Qxd5 Rad8 21. Qf3 Qxc2 22. Rac1 Qxa2 {[Strategically, the game is already decided but this is the time when I should play as simply as possible to consolidate my advantage. Instead, I relax a little over the next few moves and allow Martyn counter-chances which in turn lead to a hammer-and-tongs contest I didn't particularly want!?]} 23. Bc4 (23. Rc7 {would have been even more awkward to answer. I intended to advance my a-pawn as quickly as possible, but White has strong play against my king. (JEL Database)}) 23... Qb2 24. f5 $1 {[#]} g5 $6 ({Simpler was} 24... Kh8 $1 25. Rb1 Qd4 {with few worries, since} 26. Rb4 $2 {would then fail to} b5 $1 {winning a piece.}) ({My instinct was to play} 24... Kg7 {but I was already getting short of time and did not wish to open up any lines against my king, leading to two-edged play. As I was to realise all too soon, tactics predominate in these sort of positions, with opposite-coloured bishops and attacks on the king. (JEL Database)}) 25. Rb1 Qd4 26. Rb4 $1 Kg7 27. Qb3 $1 {[It is now White who is attacking and I have to play with great circumspection.]} b6 28. Bxf7 Qd2 29. Be6 Rd3 {[It is well known that bishops of opposite colour favour the attacker, so I decide to go into an extremely sharp but risky line in which I momentarily displace White's major pieces to launch an unusual attack.]} 30. Qa4 Qe2 31. Qxa7+ Kh8 32. Rbb1 {[#]} Rh3 $3 {[At first sight this move looks ridiculous because White is in no way going to play 33 gxh3?? Qe4+ 34 Kg1 Bd4+ 35 Rf2 Qxb1+ 36 Kg2 Qe4+ mating quickly. However, he not only has to find a defence against 33...Rxh2+! as in the game but must also deal with the immediate threat of 33...Be5! winning at once.]} 33. Rbe1 ({At first sight, it seems that} 33. Qa2 {saves White but it cuts out his later defence of Bd7 against a rook invasion down the 'd' file e.g.} Rxh2+ $1 34. Kxh2 Be5+ 35. Kg1 Bd4+ 36. Kh2 Qh5+ 37. Kg3 Qh4+ 38. Kf3 Qf4+ 39. Ke2 Qe4+ 40. Kd1 ({or} 40. Kd2 Qe3+ 41. Kc2 Qe2+ 42. Kb3 Qd3+ {mating}) 40... Rd8 $1 41. Bd5 Qd3+ 42. Ke1 Bc3+ 43. Kf2 Qd4+ 44. Kf3 Rxd5 {winning.}) (33. gxh3 $4 Qe4+ 34. Kg1 Bd4+ 35. Rf2 Qxb1+ 36. Kg2 Qe4+ {wins}) 33... Rxh2+ 34. Kxh2 Be5+ 35. Kg1 Bd4+ 36. Kh2 Qh5+ 37. Kg3 Qh4+ 38. Kf3 Qf4+ 39. Ke2 Qe3+ 40. Kd1 Qd3+ 41. Kc1 Qc3+ 42. Kd1 Rd8 $1 {[#]} 43. Bd7 $1 {[The last clever defence, involving his rook on e1, but I can just foil his plan.]} Qb3+ 44. Ke2 Qe3+ 45. Kd1 Qd3+ 46. Kc1 Be3+ $1 {[So that, if 47 Rxe3 Qxe3+ 48 Kd1 Qd4+! 49 Ke2 Rxd7 50 Qa1 Re7+ 51 Kf3 Re3+ 52 Kf2 Rc3+ winning.]} 47. Kb2 $1 {[So that after 47... Rxd7 he has 48 Qb8+ and 49 Qe5+. This forces me to guard the long black diagonal before capturing on d7, but I actually win another way.]} (47. Rxe3 Qxe3+ 48. Kd1 Qd4+ 49. Ke2 Rxd7 50. Qa1 Re7+ 51. Kf3 Re3+ 52. Kf2 Rc3+ {winning}) 47... Qd4+ $1 ({The immediate} 47... Rxd7 {allows White's queen to reach e5, as we mentioned earlier, so more manoeuvring is required! (JEL Database)}) 48. Ka3 ({The version of the game score in both BCM and CHESS continues} 48. Kb3 Qd5+ 49. Kc2 Qc4+ 50. Kb2 Bd4+ 51. Ka3 Qc3+ 52. Ka4 b5+ {0-1}) 48... Qc3+ 49. Ka4 {[Avoiding 49 Ka2 Qc2+ followed by mate but allowing ...]} b5+ {Sources: John Littlewood, ChessMoves, 2008; John Littlewood personal database (notes marked JEL Database); CHESS, February 1970, Vol.35/597-8, p179; BCM, February 1970, p60} 0-1

